  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nToday...As of 2AM, and narrow cluster of decaying storms is\ntracking east across the Twin Cities metro. After stepping \noutside our office in Chanhassen, we can confirm that there was \na good amount of lightning with it, and an even greater amount \nof bugs buzzing around. It certainly adds to our ambiance of a \nhot and humid day as dewpoints remain in the 70s for a good \nportion of the region overnight. And we'll only see Td's \nincrease today as southerly flow continues to advect more \nmoisture northwards (portions of southern MN could see Tds \nexceeding 80F by this afternoon). An Extreme Heat Warning \nremains in effect through this evening with much of the area \nexpected to see heat indices in the triple digits for several \nhours. The high end potential for heat today is greater than \nyesterday knowing this incoming airmass set all-time record \nhighs in Rapid City and produced heat indices north of 110F in \nSioux Falls. At the same time, our greater dewpoints will limit \nhow much we warm up to some extent. \n\nThe other wild card again of course is lingering cloud cover \nfrom these overnight storms. What we saw yesterday over eastern \nMN and western WI was a layer of stratus getting trapped within \nthe low-level inversion and just hanging around long enough to \nprevent those areas from warming up as much as western MN was \nable to yesterday. Early signs are showing cloud cover quickly \nclearing behind the storms, as the steering winds push it \nsoutheast. In addition to the storms and cloud cover, there are \npockets of locally dense fog this morning, especially across \nwestern WI where cloud cover has been thinner and air temps have\nbeen able to cool down to dewpoints.\n\nThe Slight Risk remains in place today for severe weather\npotential across the Upper MS Valley. The majority of hi-res \nguidance suggests the main axis of storm development today will\nbe somewhere across northeast MN and Lake Superior where the \nmid-level cap is bit weaker. Storms then slide southeast, \nlikely staying mostly across WI and MI. If we end up a few \ndegrees cooler around 700 mb, it is possible that we have storms\nfire further west into MN which would have the potential to \nproduce severe wind and hail. Timing for any storms will most \nlikely be from the late afternoon into the evening hours. \n\nMonday...We begin to \"cool off\" as the upper level ridge weakens\nand surface winds shift out of the north. This will drop \ndewpoint temperatures about 5 to 10 degrees and leave air temps \nin the low to mid 90s for areas with limited cloud cover. A Heat\nAdvisory was issued for the urban heat island of the Twin Cities\nmetro to highlight these lingering heat impacts, especially \ngiven the overnight temperatures into Monday morning will be in \nthe mid- upper 70s. The other thing that could obviously have a \nmajor impact on this would be any sort of overnight MCS like the\ndeterministic Euro wants to suggest.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...Things remain warm, but otherwise \nrelatively quiet under the northwest flow aloft. Skies will\nbecome hazy at times as elevated wildfire smoke continues to \nstream east from the western coast. There are no major signals \nfor rain outside of Thursday night into Friday as guidance \ncontinues to pinpoint a weak mid-level shortwave within the \nnorthwest flow. The latest NBM PoPs bring widespread 30 to 50 \npercent across the area which looks reasonable given the PWAT \nvalues around 1.5 to 1.8 inches and a couple thousand Joules of \nMUCAPE. Next weekend looks promising as surface high pressure \nstarts to spread in from the north and leave us in a broad area \nof subsidence.",
